> First I believe I am supposed to compile first, then patch right?

That seems backwards to me.  Ordinarily you apply patches
to source code.  Compilation[1] reads the (possibly patched)
source code and produces executable code.

> I can't seem to patch it. Again following the wiki article,
> I got eh Mingw patch files and put them in the right place
> but, I get stuck at the command prompt where the wiki
> says to type this
> -
> cd c:\nh343
> path=c:\mingw\bin
> patch -p1 < nh343-64bit.diff
> -
> All I get is a system cannot find the file specified.

You may have to change the specific paths to match the details of
where you've put things.  If you expanded the tarbal into
C:\Users\Sven\My Documents\nethack-3.4.3 then you would cd to that
instead of to C:\nh343 and similarly, if nh343-64bit.diff is not in
the current directory you would have to specify the path to it.

There's also the question of where mingw is installed.
C:\mingw\bin may or may not be correct on your system.

> I know, I'm changing the directory to nh343 (where I have the
> unpacked nh source) but as for the rest, I'm not sure what
> "path" does,

It sets the PATH environment variable, which is where command.com
(or cmd.exe if you're on NT, which come to think of it you very
probably are these days) looks for executables.  In this case,
it's looking for the patch executable, which will probably be
called patch.exe on your system.

If patch.exe is not in your path, then you have to specify the full
path to it each time you call it, like so:
C:\wherever\patch -p1 < C:\otherplace\nh343-64bit.diff

> "what -p1 <" is doing either.

The < is redirecting input from a file, hopefully from the file
containing the patch.  The -p1 is a switch option for the patch
program.  (Most software that originates in the Microsoft universe
uses forward slash to introduce switches, so the equivalent for
native Windows applications would be /p1 or more likely /p 1

However, patch was written by the same guy as Perl, and he was NOT
suffering the peculiar agony of attempting to use Microsoft software,
so naturally patch uses the hyphen to introduce switches, as is
traditional throughout the Unix multiverse.)

> Lastly the wiki says "Note that linux-style line endings will
> cause problems, you need to convert those into Windows-style ones!"
> and I have no idea what that means. or if it applies to me.

I know what it means, but I don't know whether you need to worry
about it.  Windows inherits from DOS the tradition of using a full
CRLF (carriage return and line feed: 0D0A in hex) to mark the end
of each line.  The main rationale for doing things this way is
that it closely matches the ASCII specification, which at that
time was used by a lot of hardware (including for example virtually
every text-only printer ever manufactured), which meant that you
didn't need to make any changes to your files when you wanted to
do something with them (like, say, print them): you could just
copy the file byte-for-byte to the output port (in the case of
printing, usually a parallel port) and it would Just Work.  These
considerations are essentially irrelevant now, since your file
may have who knows what Unicode characters in it and your printer
probably expects some kind of printer control or description
language (PostScript, PCL, JetDirect, maybe even PDF), but the
line ending convention hasn't really changed...

Meanwhile, Unix systems have always used just the linefeed
character (hex 0A) to indicate the end of a line.  The main reason
for doing things this way was to save one byte of disk space per
line of text file.  (Yes, this is another consideration that is
completely irrelevant on modern systems.  Unix is even older
than DOS, which in turn was older than NetHack.  A few bytes
really did matter back then.)

Most software that handles text files these days can understand
and support either line-ending format, but there are exceptions.
Notepad, notably, does not.  If you've ever downloaded a file
that was created on a Unix system and tried to open it in
Notepad only to discover that everything is one big long line,
this is why.

I'd be mildly surprised if modern versions of patch didn't
handle this issue automatically, but I could easily be wrong.

There are various programs you can get that will convert text
files (including source code and patches) from one line ending
format to another.  Emacs can be used to do it, or Perl, but
the simplest is probably to get special-purpose programs just
for this, like dos2unix and unix2dos.

Incidentally, I've found that compiling on an x64 Debian system
does not appear to require the 64bit patch.  I have no idea whether
that would also hold true on Windows, though (and I suppose it's
possible that there are runtime bugs I don't know about).

